%[text] %[text:anchor:T_tp0] # Estimate Totem-Pole Boost PFC Converter Parameters
%[text] This script sizes the power stage of a bridgeless totem-pole boost power factor correction (PFC) converter. From the AC input voltage range, the line frequency, the DC bus voltage, the rated power, the switching frequency and the allowed ripple, it estimates the boost inductance and the DC-bus capacitance, reports the device and magnetics current ratings across the input range, and verifies the conduction mode.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ov] ## Overview
%[text] The totem-pole PFC has a single boost inductor on the AC line and a high-frequency "fast leg" half-bridge switching at $f\_{sw}${"editStyle":"visual"} . A "slow leg" half-bridge commutates at the line frequency to select the return path by line polarity. With no input rectifier bridge, two diode forward drops leave the conduction path, which improves efficiency.
%[text] In continuous conduction the volt-second balance over a switching period gives the duty of the main switch, and with the modulation index $m=V\_{ac,pk} /V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} :
%[text] $\\left|v\_{ac} \\left(t\\right)\\right|=\\left(1-d\\left(t\\right)\\right)V\_{out} \\;\\Longrightarrow \\;d\\left(t\\right)=1-m\\sin \\theta$
%[text] A totem-pole boost converter cannot buck, so $m\<1${"editStyle":"visual"} is required at the highest line voltage. This script helps you:
%[text] 1. Check boost feasibility and compute the operating point at each AC input voltage.
%[text] 2. Size the boost inductance $L${"editStyle":"visual"} from the worst-case ripple over the line cycle, taking the largest requirement across the input range.
%[text] 3. Verify the continuous-conduction-mode (CCM) margin and report the power below which conduction goes discontinuous.
%[text] 4. Size the DC-bus capacitance $C\_o${"editStyle":"visual"} as the larger of the second-harmonic ripple requirement and the hold-up requirement.
%[text] 5. Report the device and magnetics current rating at the lowest line voltage. \
%[text] For equations and definitions, see the [Annexure](internal:H_annex).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_rate] ## Specify Converter Rating
%[text] Specify the totem-pole PFC ratings. The current is highest at the minimum RMS line voltage. Boost feasibility, the inductance requirement and the CCM margin are all worst at the maximum.
totemPoleSpec.minInputVoltage = simscape.Value(207,"V"); % Minimum AC input voltage (RMS)
totemPoleSpec.nomInputVoltage = simscape.Value(230,"V"); % Nominal AC input voltage (RMS)
totemPoleSpec.maxInputVoltage = simscape.Value(264,"V"); % Maximum AC input voltage (RMS)
totemPoleSpec.lineFrequency   = simscape.Value(50,"Hz"); % AC line frequency

totemPoleSpec.outputVoltage  = simscape.Value(400,"V");  % Regulated DC bus voltage
totemPoleSpec.ratedPower     = simscape.Value(3000,"W"); % Rated (maximum) output power
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_sw] ## Specify Switching Frequency and Allowed Ripple
%[text] The switching frequency and the allowed inductor current ripple together set the boost inductance. The ripple fraction is the peak-to-peak high-frequency ripple as a fraction of the peak line current, taken at its worst point over the line cycle.
%[text] The ripple fraction $\\left(r\_i \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} also sets the CCM load range at the AC voltage peak, $P\_{CCM} /P\_{rated} =2r\_i m${"editStyle":"visual"}. A ripple fraction of 0.3 at $m=0\\ldotp 93${"editStyle":"visual"} means discontinuous conduction below about 56% of rated power. Raising the switching frequency does not widen that range, because holding the same ripple fraction shrinks the inductance in exact proportion.
%[text] The DC-bus ripple fraction is likewise peak-to-peak, as a fraction of the output voltage. This ripple appears at twice the line frequency, not at the switching frequency, and is irreducible for a single-phase PFC. The PFC therefore requires large bus capacitors.
totemPoleSpec.switchingFrequency = simscape.Value(100e3,"Hz"); % Fast-leg switching frequency
totemPoleSpec.inductorCurrentRippleFraction = 0.30; % Peak-to-peak inductor ripple as a fraction of peak line current
totemPoleSpec.outputVoltageRippleFraction = 0.02; % Peak-to-peak DC-bus ripple at 2*fLine (fraction of Vout)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_hold] ## Specify Output Capacitor Hold-Up
%[text] If the mains is lost, the DC-bus capacitor alone must hold the bus above a floor voltage for a ride-through time at rated power. This sag is large, so the capacitance follows from the stored energy balance rather than a linear discharge approximation. If ride-through is not required, set the hold-up time to zero.
totemPoleSpec.holdUpTime       = simscape.Value(8e-3,"s"); % Ride-through time at rated power after loss of mains
totemPoleSpec.minHoldUpVoltage = simscape.Value(340,"V");  % Bus voltage floor during hold-up
%[text] The final capacitance is the larger of the ripple and hold-up requirements. With the values above, the ripple requirement governs; a longer hold-up time or a lower floor voltage makes hold-up govern instead, and the design reports which one does.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_marg] ## Specify Design Margins
%[text] The boost margin is how far the DC bus must sit above the highest AC peak. At a margin of one, the converter has no duty range left at the AC peak, so the margin must exceed one. The device voltage margin only sets the recommended device rating; every device in this topology blocks the full DC bus.
totemPoleSpec.boostVoltageMargin  = 1.05; % Required ratio of Vout to the maximum AC peak
totemPoleSpec.deviceVoltageMargin = 1.30; % Device voltage rating as a multiple of the DC bus
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_para] ## Specify Parasitic Parameters
%[text] Parasitic resistances affect the device currents and the efficiency. Set them from component datasheets. The slow-leg diode forward voltage only reports the conduction loss that the bridgeless topology saves compared with a conventional bridge PFC.
%[text] The DC-bus capacitor starts at the regulated bus voltage, which skips the inrush and boost-up transient, so the results settle within a few line cycles. For a startup study, set it to 0 V and add an inrush-limiting element.
totemPoleSpec.inductorESR  = simscape.Value(0.02,"Ohm"); % Boost inductor series resistance
totemPoleSpec.capacitorESR = simscape.Value(0.01,"Ohm"); % DC-bus capacitor series resistance
totemPoleSpec.initialCapacitorVoltage = totemPoleSpec.outputVoltage; % DC-bus capacitor initial voltage
totemPoleSpec.slowLegDiodeForwardVoltage = simscape.Value(0.7,"V"); % Rectifier drop avoided by going bridgeless
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_est] ## Estimate Totem-Pole Boost PFC Parameters
%[text] Compute the boost inductance, the DC-bus capacitance and the CCM margin. `DisplayTableFlag` shows the main design table here. `SelectTotemPoleBoostPFCComponents` gives the device voltage and current ratings.
%[text] `TotemPoleBoostPFCDefaultInputParameters` computes the same values, so the model also opens without this script.
[designTable, totemPoleDesign] = designTotemPoleBoostPFCConverter(totemPoleSpec, ... %[output:group:82d6997a] %[output:40170e55]
    "DisplayTableFlag", true); %[output:group:82d6997a] %[output:40170e55]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ripple] ## Verify Inductor Current Ripple and Peak Current
%[text] Plot the high-frequency ripple across a line half cycle at every AC input voltage, together with the inductor current envelope at the highest-current input voltage. The plot shows the two facts that drive the inductor sizing.
%[text] First, the ripple $\\Delta I\_L =\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|\\left(1-\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|/V\_{out} \\right)/\\left(Lf\_{sw} \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} is quadratic in the instantaneous AC voltage, so it peaks where $\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|=V\_{out} /2${"editStyle":"visual"} and not at the AC peak. The circles mark the true worst case and the crosses mark the value at the AC peak; the gap between them is what an at-AC-peak sizing rule misses. Because the worst-case value $V\_{out} /\\left(4Lf\_{sw} \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} contains no AC voltage term, all three curves reach the same maximum. The ripple fraction is therefore worst at the highest line voltage where the peak current is smallest.
%[text] Second, the envelope $i\_L \\pm \\Delta I\_L /2${"editStyle":"visual"} sets the core saturation rating from its upper edge and the CCM boundary from its lower edge. The marked peak is the true numerical maximum, which for large ripple fractions moves off the AC peak.
plotTotemPoleInductorCurrentRipple(totemPoleDesign); %[output:5d7853ba]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ccm] ## Verify Conduction Mode Across the Input Range
%[text] Requiring $i\_L \>\\Delta I\_L /2${"editStyle":"visual"} at every point of the line cycle causes the $\\sin \\theta${"editStyle":"visual"} to cancel exactly; CCM is hardest at high line.
%[text] Above the boundary the converter is in CCM over the whole line cycle; below it, conduction goes discontinuous near the zero crossings. Some light-load DCM is unavoidable, because CCM down to zero load requires an infinite inductance. The plot shows how much of the load range is continuous.
plotTotemPoleCCMBoundary(totemPoleDesign); %[output:73de61e3]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ctrl] ## Specify Controller Parameters
%[text] Tune the average-current-mode controller against the sized power stage. An outer voltage PI trims a feedforward power reference. The commanded power multiplied by the per-unit line voltage forms the per-unit current reference that the inner current loop tracks.
totemPoleControllerSpec.phaseMargin           = 60; % Target phase margin, both loops (deg)
totemPoleControllerSpec.currentCrossoverRatio = 20; % Current-loop crossover as Fsw/ratio
totemPoleControllerSpec.voltageCrossoverFrequency = simscape.Value(3,"Hz"); % Low to reject 100 Hz bus ripple
totemPoleControllerSpec.iRefPeakMaxPu = 1.2; % Peak current-reference limit, pu of the rated line-current peak
totemPoleControllerSpec.loadFraction  = 0.5; % Power command as a fraction of rated power
totemPoleControllerSpec.gateVoltageOn  = simscape.Value(15,"V"); % Gate drive ON level
totemPoleControllerSpec.gateVoltageOff = simscape.Value(0,"V");  % Gate drive OFF level
%[text] The quantities the controller shares with the plant - AC input voltage, DC bus voltage, rated power and switching frequency - come from `totemPoleDesign`.
[controllerTable, totemPoleController] = designTotemPoleBoostPFCController(totemPoleDesign, ... %[output:group:5e8c04a2] %[output:9f2a1b73]
    totemPoleControllerSpec, DisplayTableFlag = true); %[output:group:5e8c04a2] %[output:9f2a1b73]
%[text] The two dead times are gate-driver hardware limits, not estimates, so set them directly.
totemPoleController.fastLegDeadTime    = 300e-9; % Fast-leg dead time on both edges (s)
totemPoleController.slowLegTurnOnDelay = 10e-6; % Slow-leg turn-on delay after the line zero crossing (s)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_annex] ## Annexure: Equations and Definitions
%[text] **Boost feasibility and duty.** A boost converter cannot buck, so with $V\_{pk} =\\sqrt{2}V\_{rms}${"editStyle":"visual"} and $m=V\_{pk} /V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} the bus must satisfy $V\_{out} \\ge \\textrm{boostVoltageMargin}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}V\_{ac,\\max }${"editStyle":"visual"} . The CCM duty of the main switch and its minimum are:
%[text] $d\\left(\\theta \\right)=1-m\\sin \\theta \\;,\\quad d\_{\\min } =1-m\\;\\textrm{at}\\;\\theta =\\pi /2$
%[text] **Line currents (unity power factor).** The single boost inductor sits on the line, so it carries the whole input current:
%[text] $I\_{rms} =\\frac{P\_{out} }{V\_{rms} }\\;,\\quad I\_{pk} =\\sqrt{2}I\_{rms} \\;,\\quad i\_L \\left(\\theta \\right)=I\_{pk} \\sin \\theta$
%[text] **Inductor ripple over the line cycle.** The ripple is quadratic in $\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|${"editStyle":"visual"} and therefore peaks at $\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|=V\_{out} /2${"editStyle":"visual"} , not at the AC peak:
%[text] $\\Delta I\_L \\left(\\theta \\right)=\\frac{\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|d}{Lf\_{sw} }\\;\\Longrightarrow \\;\\Delta I\_{L,\\max } =\\frac{V\_{out} }{4Lf\_{sw} }\\;\\;\\left(V\_{pk} \\ge V\_{out} /2\\right)$
%[text] **Boost inductance.** Requiring $\\Delta I\_{L,\\max } \\le r\_i I\_{pk}${"editStyle":"visual"} and inverting at each AC input voltage, then taking the largest requirement (which lands on the maximum line voltage, where $I\_{pk}${"editStyle":"visual"} is smallest):
%[text] $L=\\max\_{V\_{ac} } \\frac{V\_{out} }{4f\_{sw} r\_i I\_{pk} }$
%[text] **Continuous conduction.** Requiring $i\_L \>\\Delta I\_L /2${"editStyle":"visual"} everywhere, the $\\sin \\theta${"editStyle":"visual"} cancels exactly and leaves a line-phase-independent criterion, plus the power below which conduction goes discontinuous near the zero crossings:
%[text] $L\_{CCM} =\\frac{V\_{pk} }{2f\_{sw} I\_{pk} }\\;,\\quad P\_{CCM} =\\frac{V\_{rms}^2 }{2f\_{sw} L}$
%[text] **The ripple fraction and the CCM range are one degree of freedom.** Substituting the sized $L${"editStyle":"visual"} above into both CCM results collapses them to exact reciprocal identities containing neither $f\_{sw}${"editStyle":"visual"} , $L${"editStyle":"visual"} nor the power level:
%[text] $\\frac{L}{L\_{CCM} }=\\frac{1}{2r\_i m}\\;,\\quad \\frac{P\_{CCM} }{P\_{rated} }=2r\_i m\\;\\Longrightarrow \\;\\textrm{full}-\\textrm{cycle}\\;\\textrm{CCM}\\;\\textrm{needs}\\;r\_i \<\\frac{1}{2m}$
%[text] **Peak inductor current (core saturation).** The envelope maximum is evaluated numerically, because the usual at-AC-peak closed form under-predicts the true peak once the ripple fraction approaches unity:
%[text] $I\_{L,pk} \\approx I\_{pk} +\\frac{V\_{pk} \\left(1-m\\right)}{2Lf\_{sw} }\\;\\;\\left(\\textrm{exact}\\;\\textrm{only}\\;\\textrm{for}\\;\\textrm{moderate}\\;\\textrm{ripple}\\right)$
%[text] **DC-bus capacitance.** A single-phase PFC has an input power $P\_{in} \\left(1-\\cos 2\\omega\_{line} t\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} against a constant load, so the capacitor absorbs the whole pulsating term. Integrating gives a ripple amplitude of $P/\\left(2\\omega\_{line} C\_o V\_{out} \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} , and a peak-to-peak ripple twice that. With the hold-up requirement from the energy balance, the capacitance is the larger of:
%[text] $C\_{o,\\textrm{ripple}} =\\frac{P\_{out} }{\\omega\_{line} r\_v V\_{out}^2 }\\;,\\quad C\_{o,\\textrm{holdup}} =\\frac{2P\_{out} t\_{\\textrm{hold}} }{V\_{out}^2 -V\_{\\min }^2 }\\;,\\quad I\_{Co,rms} =\\frac{P\_{out} }{\\sqrt{2}V\_{out} }$
%[text] **Fast-leg conduction currents.** Integrating $i\_L =I\_{pk} \\sin \\theta${"editStyle":"visual"} with $d=1-m\\sin \\theta${"editStyle":"visual"} over a line half cycle gives exact closed forms for the main (boost) switch and the rectifier device:
%[text] $I\_{Q,rms} =I\_{pk} \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4m}{3\\pi }}\\;,\\quad I\_{D,rms} =I\_{pk} \\sqrt{\\frac{4m}{3\\pi }}\\;,\\quad I\_{D,avg} =\\frac{I\_{pk} m}{2}=\\frac{P\_{in} }{V\_{out} }$
%[text] Two exact identities make these self-checking: $I\_{Q,rms}^2 +I\_{D,rms}^2 =I\_{L,rms}^2${"editStyle":"visual"} (the duty splits one current) and $I\_{D,avg} =P\_{out} /V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} (the rectifier feeds the bus). Over a full line cycle each fast-leg device alternates between the two roles, so each carries $I\_{pk} /2${"editStyle":"visual"} .
%[text] **Slow-leg conduction currents.** Each slow-leg device conducts the line current for one half cycle:
%[text] $I\_{slow,rms} =\\frac{I\_{pk} }{2}\\;,\\quad I\_{slow,avg} =\\frac{I\_{pk} }{\\pi }\\;,\\quad I\_{slow,pk} =I\_{pk}$
%[text] **Bridgeless advantage.** A conventional bridge-plus-boost PFC keeps two rectifier drops in the conduction path at all times. The mean rectified line current is $2I\_{pk} /\\pi${"editStyle":"visual"} , so the totem-pole saves:
%[text] $P\_{\\textrm{saved}} =2V\_f \\frac{2I\_{pk} }{\\pi }$
%[text] **Not sized here.** The dead time is not derivable as it is for a dual active bridge: the fast leg is hard-switched in CCM and the inductor current never reverses, so there is no resonant zero-voltage transition to invert. A $\\pi \\sqrt{2C\_{oss} L}${"editStyle":"visual"} expression from a DAB design does not apply. The EMI filter, the fast-leg body-diode reverse recovery, inrush limiting and thermal design are also out of scope.
%[text] ### Variable Definitions
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nV\_{rms} ,V\_{pk} -\\textrm{AC}\\;\\textrm{input}\\;\\textrm{voltage},\\;\\textrm{RMS}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{peak}\\\\\nV\_{out} -\\textrm{Regulated}\\;\\textrm{DC}\\;\\textrm{bus}\\;\\textrm{voltage}\\\\\nm=V\_{pk} /V\_{out} -\\textrm{Modulation}\\;\\textrm{index}\\;\\left(m\<1\\;\\textrm{required}\\right)\\\\\nd\\left(\\theta \\right)-\\textrm{CCM}\\;\\textrm{duty}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;\\textrm{the}\\;\\textrm{main}\\;\\left(\\textrm{boost}\\right)\\;\\textrm{switch}\\\\\n\\theta =\\omega\_{line} t-\\textrm{Line}\\;\\textrm{phase};\\;\\textrm{a}\\;\\textrm{half}\\;\\textrm{cycle}\\;\\textrm{is}\\;\\theta \\in \\left\\lbrack 0,\\pi \\right\\rbrack \\\\\nL-\\textrm{Boost}\\;\\textrm{inductance}\\;\\left(\\textrm{plant}\\;\\textrm{LBoost}\\right)\\\\\nC\_o -\\textrm{DC}-\\textrm{bus}\\;\\textrm{capacitance}\\;\\left(\\textrm{plant}\\;\\textrm{CBoost}\\right)\\\\\nf\_{sw} -\\textrm{Fast}-\\textrm{leg}\\;\\textrm{switching}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\nf\_{line} ,\\omega\_{line} -\\textrm{Line}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{its}\\;\\textrm{angular}\\;\\textrm{value}\\\\\nI\_{pk} ,I\_{rms} -\\textrm{Peak}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{RMS}\\;\\textrm{line}\\;\\textrm{current}\\\\\nr\_i -\\textrm{Peak}-\\textrm{to}-\\textrm{peak}\\;\\textrm{inductor}\\;\\textrm{ripple}\\;\\textrm{as}\\;\\textrm{a}\\;\\textrm{fraction}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;I\_{pk} \\\\\nr\_v -\\textrm{Peak}-\\textrm{to}-\\textrm{peak}\\;\\textrm{DC}-\\textrm{bus}\\;\\textrm{ripple}\\;\\textrm{as}\\;\\textrm{a}\\;\\textrm{fraction}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;V\_{out} \n\\end{array}$
%%
